  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">U.S. historical term used to describe the practices of individuals who wear clothes associated with a gender other than their gender assigned at birth; generally considered to be a derogatory term; use only in historical contexts</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">U.S. historical term used to describe individuals who wear clothes associated with a gender other than their gender assigned at birth; generally considered to be a derogatory term; use only in historical contexts</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Spanish term for people assigned male at birth who have a feminine gender identity; commonly used in South America</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Historical term for individuals who engage in the sexual practice of tribadism; use only in historical contexts</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gender identity including three genders, typically male, female, and a third (often non-binary) gender</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People whose gender continually moves between or among three gender identities, typically male, female, and a third gender</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Ancient Hindu term for a third gender that is typically described as fluid and/or continuously transformative</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Using various methods to visually conceal male genitalia to give a flatter appearance in clothing</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Israeli term used in reference to an unknown gender; literally translates to "hidden"</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Self-identifying term used by indigenous people in North America to reconnect with the multiple gender and sexual roles in Native cultures</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">19th century term used to refer to an LGBTQ person; use only in historical contexts</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Any non-surgical technique used to make the human voice sound more feminine or masculine</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In M&#x101;ori culture in New Zealand, people assigned female at birth who pursue traditionally male roles</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Third gender people in Indonesia who are assigned male at birth but who express femininity and may identify as women</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In M&#x101;ori culture in New Zealand, people assigned male at birth who pursue traditionally female roles</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Stick, cord, or strap, usually with a stiff handle, that is used for striking an individual</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A feminist spelling of &#x201C;women&#x201D;. First used by George P. Krapp (1872-1934), and then appearing in 19th-century Black American English renderings before being used by feminists in the 20th-century, such as by the fictional character Millie Tant.&#xD;
</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">North American Lakota word referring to people assigned male at birth who adopt feminine roles; translates to "to be like a woman"</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Heavy-set, masculine people who are often characterised as having hairy bodies and facial hair; typically used for individuals who are more aggressive and overtly masculine than their counterparts, bears</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Women who engage in sexual activity with other women</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Same-gender marriage tradition between two women in Africa conducted according to African culture and traditions</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A feminist spelling of &#x201C;woman&#x201D; or &#x201C;women&#x201D; first appearing in print in 1976. Note that some contemporary transgender activists have criticized use of the word  &#x201C;womyn" due to its prevalence in trans-exclusionary radical feminist (TERF) circles following the exclusion of trans women from the Michigan Womyn&#x2019;s Music Festival. Based on an Older Scots spelling of woman.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Arabic word used in Oman for people who assigned male at birth who adopt feminine mannerisms and/or roles</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Japanese term for people who do not fit within the categories of man or woman; includes individuals born intersex, female, or male, and is often abbreviated using FTX, MTX, or XTX to describe individuals' transitions; some definitions equate the term to genderqueer or beyond the binary identities</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Chinese word for an individual who is both female (yin) and male (yang)</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The time period between childhood and adulthood</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In northern India and Pakistan, this term is used to refer to effeminate people</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Urdu word for effeminate people</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A sexual orientation generally characterized by feeling sexual attraction or a desire for partnered sexuality</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The assumption that all people share a desire for romantic relationships and that this is a preferential life goal</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The belief that everyone should be either sexually available or in a sexual relationship.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In polyamory or open relationships, refers to the partner of an individual's partner's, with whom the individual does not have a direct sexual or romantic relationship</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The belief that all people share a desire for monogamous relationships and that this is a preferential life goal</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A sexuality that involves attraction to only one gender; includes homosexuality and heterosexuality</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A sexuality that involves attraction to more than one gender, such as bisexuality or pansexuality</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Terms that have been or are currently used in derogatory ways but have been reclaimed by certain groups or in certain circumstances</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Insulting or discriminating remarks, innuendos, or names intended to demoralize a person, which are usually based on that person's belonging to a particular social group. Typically, for a term to be considered a slur, it must be derogatory towards a particular person or group, it must be used to subordinate them within some structure of power relations, and the derogated person or group must be defined by an intrinsic property (such as race, sexual orientation, gender identity, etc.). This category includes terms considered slurs at any point in their usage. Therefore, some of these terms may no longer be considered necessarily derogatory, may be in common usage, or may be selectively reclaimed by certain groups</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Umbrella term used to describe individuals who trans gender regardless of their identification; sometimes used as a way to historicize non-binary identities</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Historical term from the German "Uranismus" coined by Karl Heinrich Ulrichs as an umbrella term for what are now referred to as gender and sexual minorities; use only in historical contexts</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A surgical procedure aimed at increasing breast size using a prosthetic implant; often undergone by transgender women as a gender confirming surgery</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A surgical procedure to decrease breast size</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Don&#x2019;t Ask Don&#x2019;t Tell Relationships, where partners are (by agreement) allowed to have multiple romantic and/or sexual relationships as long as they do not discuss it with their primary partner or let it interfere with their partner&#x2019;s daily life</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A sexual orientation generally characterized by infrequent, rare, or low-intensity sexual attraction</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who experience infrequent, rare, or low-intensity sexual attraction</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term used in the swinging community to describe a type of partner swapping where sexual activities happen in different spaces or rooms where partners may not necessarily be in sight of each other</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A form of consensual nonmonogamy in which a person has multiple partners who are not "equal" to one another in terms of time, emotional intensity, financial, or scheduling matters</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Breasts considered large within a given cultural context. Note that large breasts have various health issues associated with them and may carry stigmatized medical associations (such as terms like gigantomastia and macromastia). These terms have more specific medical meanings but should not be used outside of those specific contexts. Gigantomastia is breast hypertrophy in which tissue considered excessive weighs more than 1.5 kilograms (3.3 lbs); macromastia is hypertrophy in which the tissue weighs less than 1.5 kilograms (3.3 lbs)</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A surgical procedure aimed at removing the breast or as much breast tissue as possible; often undergone as part of "top surgery" for transgender men or some nonbinary people</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A sexual orientation generally characterized by a person's lack of emotional connection to other people unless or until they have formed a strong sexual connection with them first</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who do not experience strong emotional connections to other people unless they form strong sexual connections with them first</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Any of various fetishes regarding a specific part of the body</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term used in hierarchical polyamory to refer to the partner whose interests and desires come before any secondary partner(s)</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An individual's preference toward or away from a particular relationship model</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People whose relationship practices differ from those of the dominant culture</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term used in hierarchical polyamory to refer to the partner whose interests and desires come second to those of the primary partner(s)</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Breasts considered small within a given cultural context. In certain situations, medical professionals may refer to such breasts as "underdeveloped" or "hypoplastic." These terms can be considered offensive and should not be used outside of specific medical contexts. The alternate labels included here are intended to aid search tools only and should not be used in other contexts</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term used in the swinging community to describe a type of partner swapping where sexual activities happen in the same shared space so that all partners are within sight of one another</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An agreement, sexual practice, or relationship in which a couple engages in group sex or the swapping of sexual partners within a group, often on a habitual basis</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A romantic orientation generally characterized by a lack of romantic attraction or desire&#xD;
</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term used in non-monogamous communities to refer to a long-term partner with multiple commitments. Often used in place of language seen as hierarchical (e.g. primary/secondary partner).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An assignment of an "X" gender marker or a male sex designation shortly after birth.&#xD;
</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A violation of a couple's assumed or stated contract regarding emotional and/or sexual exclusivity. Should not be used to refer to non-monogamy in general, as cheating is possible in both monogamous and non-monogamous relationships.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An individual's sex as determined by a medical practitioner or provider. Determination is usually based on the appearance of external genitalia but may use other methodologies. In practice, sex assigned at birth is often culturally variable; the term "assigned gender" is often more applicable or accurate.&#xD;
</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The positive emotion an individual may experience in regards to their partner's relationship with another partner. Often defined as the opposite of jealousy.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The assumption that all people share a desire for monogamous relationships and that this is a preferential life goal.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Practice of romantic or sexual relationships with more than one person simultaneously and with all the consent of all partners involved.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A gender identity describing someone who partially, but not fully, identifies as a woman or girl irrespective of their assigned gender. Used by cisgender, transgender, transfeminine, or nonbinary people who feel more strongly associated with women physically or socially, but not strongly enough to want to identify as one.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A gender identity describing someone who partially, but not fully, identifies as a man or boy irrespective of their assigned gender. Used by cisgender, transgender, transmasculine, or nonbinary people who feel more strongly associated with men physically or socially, but not strongly enough to want to identify as one.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay subculture popularized in the 1970s via homoerotic bodybuilder magazines. Often includes aesthetics modeled after ancient Greek gymnasiums. Especially popular in San Francisco, West Hollywood, and New York City.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A period of time in a relationship characterized by passionate love as well as intense feelings of attraction and ecstasy, sometimes accompanied by idealization of a partner or the shared relationship.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A relationship that is mostly monogamous with occasional exceptions made for sexual play.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term used in non-monogamous communities to refer to a partner that one lives with. Often used in place of language seen as hierarchical (e.g. primary/secondary partner).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Practice of romantic or sexual relationships with more than one person simultaneously, without consent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Practice of romantic or sexual relationships with more than one person simultaneously. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who practice romantic or sexual relationships with more than one person simultaneously.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A strong, almost giddy feeling of excitement and infatuation common in the beginning of a new romantic relationship. Often used in polyamory contexts.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In polyamory contexts, refers to one's (non-consensual) partner's partner. In other contexts, used more broadly to refer to any lover.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who practice relationships with more than one person, simultaneously, with the knowledge and consent of all parties.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In polyamory, a multiple-partner relationship that is 'closed off' to new participants per the agreement of those involved.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">People who practice a type of non-monogamy characterized by application of anarchist principles (such as autonomy, anti-normativity, anti-hierarchical practices, refusal of state governance) to relationships. Often contrasted with hierarchical polyamory.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A type of non-monogamy characterized by application of anarchist principles (such as autonomy, anti-normativity, anti-hierarchical practices, refusal of state governance) to relationships. Often contrasted with hierarchical polyamory.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Typically, a sexual activity in which two women rub their genitalia against one &#xD;
another while intertwining their legs.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Someone who is both trans and genderqueer and who identifies as transgenderqueer.&#xD;
</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A mostly antiquated term used either to refer to scissoring, to any sexual activity between women, or to the general concept of being a lesbian ("lesbianism").</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Non-binary gender identity that is not defined in relation to human understandings of gender (i.e., female or male), but instead in relation to animals, plants, or other things.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A person who identifies their gender in relation to non-human understandings of gender and relates to animals, plants, or other things.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Personal pronouns that are not officially (i.e., in dictionaries, style guides, etc.) recognized by the language that they are used in. Often intended to be gender neutral.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A pronoun. Used in this manner: Ae went to the Homosaurus website. I watched aer browse the Homosaurus. Ae uses the Homosaurus aerself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A pronoun. Used in this manner: Co went to the Homosaurus website. I watched cos browse the Homosaurus. Co uses the Homosaurus coself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
